   "unit": "Foundations",
   "quickIdea": "A truth table lists every interpretation. If every row is true the formula is a tautology, if every row is false it is a contradiction, and anything else is a contingency.",
   "remember": [
    "A formula with n variables has 2^n rows: 4 for two variables, 8 for three.",
    "Tautology: true in every row. Contradiction: false in every row. Contingency (satisfiable and falsifiable): true in some rows and false in others.",
    "There are 2^(2^n) distinct truth functions of n variables: 16 of two variables, 256 of three.",
    "To prove a tautology, every row must be true - one falsifying row is enough to disprove it.",
    "Two formulas are equivalent exactly when they have identical columns, so one truth table settles an equivalence question.",
    "Column order: build innermost brackets first, then work outwards."
   ],
   "example": {
    "prompt": "Is (not p and (p or q)) -> q a tautology?",
    "work": "not p and (p or q) simplifies to not p and q. Then (not p and q) -> q is equivalent to p or not q or q, which is true regardless of p and q.",
    "answer": "Yes, a tautology. Both rows that could falsify it fail: the antecedent is true only when p is false and q is true, and there q is true."
   },
   "gateAngle": "GATE 2021 Set 1 Q.7 gives two formulas and asks which is a tautology: S1 = (not p and (p or q)) -> q is one, S2 = q -> (not p and (p or q)) is not, because it fails at p = q = true. The exam wants the falsifying row, not a full table.",
   "variations": [
    {
     "label": "ORIGINAL PYQ-STYLE QUESTION",
     "question": "How many rows does the truth table of a formula in p, q, r, s have, and how many distinct truth functions of three variables exist?",
     "answer": "16 rows; 256 truth functions",
     "why": "Rows = 2^4 = 16. Truth functions of n variables = 2^(2^n), so for n = 3 that is 2^8 = 256."
    },
    {
     "label": "PREDICTED VARIATION",
     "question": "Is (p -> q) or (q -> p) a tautology?",
     "answer": "Yes",
     "why": "If p is false then p -> q is true. If p is true then q -> p is q -> true, which is true. So one disjunct is always true, and a disjunction with a true disjunct is true."
    }
   ],
