   "id": "rules-of-inference",
   "title": "Rules of Inference and Argument Validity",
   "priority": "P1",
   "order": 23,
   "unit": "Proof",
   "quickIdea": "An argument is valid when the premises cannot all be true while the conclusion is false. A short list of inference rules covers almost every GATE argument question.",
   "remember": [
    "Argument form: premises above the line, conclusion below. It is valid when (premise1 and ... and premisen) -> conclusion is a tautology.",
    "Modus ponens: p, p -> q, therefore q. Modus tollens: not q, p -> q, therefore not p.",
    "Hypothetical syllogism: p -> q, q -> r, therefore p -> r.",
    "Disjunctive syllogism: p or q, not p, therefore q. Addition: p, therefore p or q. Simplification: p and q, therefore p.",
    "Resolution: p or r, q or not r, therefore p or q.",
    "Fastest test: make the conclusion false, then try to satisfy every premise. If you cannot, the argument is valid.",
    "Two named fallacies: affirming the consequent (p -> q, q, therefore p) and denying the antecedent (p -> q, not p, therefore not q). Both are invalid."
   ],
   "example": {
    "prompt": "Premises: if it rains the match is not played; it rains. What follows, and is the argument valid?",
    "work": "R = it rains, P = the match is played. Premises are R -> not P and R; the conclusion not P follows by modus ponens. Making not P false forces P true, and then R -> not P is false while R is true, so no model makes the premises true and the conclusion false.",
    "answer": "Conclusion: the match is not played. The argument is valid."
   },
   "gateAngle": "GATE 2015 Set 2 Q.3 gives two statements and asks what follows by sound inference: from C -> not E and K -> E, the contrapositive E -> not C chains with K -> E to give K -> not C. The answer is the statement 'if a person is kind, he is not known to be corrupt'.",
   "variations": [
    {
     "label": "ORIGINAL PYQ-STYLE QUESTION",
     "question": "Premises: p -> q and not q. What follows?",
     "answer": "not p, by modus tollens",
     "why": "If p were true, modus ponens would give q, contradicting not q. So p must be false. This is the contrapositive of the premise applied to not q."
    },
    {
     "label": "CONCEPTUAL PRACTICE QUESTION",
     "question": "Name the fallacy in: p -> q, q, therefore p.",
     "answer": "Affirming the consequent - invalid",
     "why": "The row p = false, q = true makes both premises true and the conclusion false, so the argument has a counterexample model and is invalid."
    }
   ],
